   582	## Claim: Two-arm fast/slow delay test reduces to delayed advantage control
   583	Status: proven as a reduction; target bound remains conjectural
   584	Confidence: high for the algebraic reduction, low-medium for the desired theorem
   585	Dependencies: Full-information two-arm rewards \(f_t,s_t\in[0,1]\); fast arm has immediate feedback, slow arm has delay \(D\); learner plays slow with probability \(q_t\); expected regret against always-fast and always-slow.
   586	Evidence: Let
   587	\[
   588	\Delta_t=s_t-f_t,\qquad S_T=\sum_t\Delta_t,\qquad A_T=\sum_t q_t\Delta_t.
   589	\]
   590	The learner's reward relative to always-fast is \(A_T\), and always-slow exceeds always-fast by \(S_T\). Hence
   591	\[
   592	R_F=-A_T,\qquad R_S=S_T-A_T.
   593	\]
   594	Thus the desired simultaneous bounds reduce to
   595	\[
   596	A_T\ge -B_F,\qquad A_T\ge S_T-B_S,
   597	\]
   598	with \(B_F\) on the order of \(\sqrt T\) and \(B_S\) on the order of \(\sqrt{TD}\).
