   640	## Lemma: Horizon-censored nested-window increments
   641	Status: proved
   642	Assumptions: Public exogenous finite horizon \(T\); fixed quote \(b\); dyadic or increasing windows \(0=H_0<\cdots<H_M=T\); truncated rewards
   643	\[
   644	g_t^m(b)=r_t^{H_m}(b)=b{\bf 1}\{\tau_t(b)\le H_m,\ \tau_t(b)\le T-t\}.
   645	\]
   646	Statement: The increments
   647	\[
   648	z_t^m(b)=g_t^m(b)-g_t^{m-1}(b)
   649	\]
   650	satisfy
   651	\[
   652	z_t^m(b)=b{\bf 1}\{H_{m-1}<\tau_t(b)\le H_m,\ \tau_t(b)\le T-t\},
   653	\]
   654	are nonnegative and disjoint in \(m\) for fixed \((t,b)\), and are revealed after delay at most \(H_m\).
   655	Proof sketch: Subtract adjacent horizon-censored indicators. A realized first hit \(\tau_t(b)\) falls in at most one interval \((H_{m-1},H_m]\), and if it exceeds \(T-t\) it yields no finite-horizon reward.
   656	Known gaps: This is only structural. It does not imply a nested-window adaptive algorithm; pathwise comparator charges and expected \(\delta_T^P(H)\) must not be mixed.
   657	Source: `iterations/iter_014/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_014/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
