   674	## Lemma: Independent-bit comparator collapse in an additive two-quote block model
   675	Status: proved for the idealized additive model; not a market realization theorem
   676	Assumptions: There are independent symmetric signs \(\sigma_j\in\{\pm1\}\), block weights \(w_j\ge0\), and a single fixed comparator chooses between two quotes whose relative advantage across all blocks is \(\sum_j w_j\sigma_j\).
   677	Statement:
   678	\[
   679	\mathbb E_\sigma\left|\sum_j w_j\sigma_j\right|
   680	\le
   681	\left(\sum_jw_j^2\right)^{1/2}.
   682	\]
   683	Thus equal independent hidden-bit blocks give only \(w\sqrt N\) expected comparator advantage, not \(Nw\).
   684	Proof sketch: Jensen and independence give
   685	\[
   686	\mathbb E|S|\le (\mathbb E S^2)^{1/2}
   687	=\left(\sum_jw_j^2\right)^{1/2}.
   688	\]
   689	Known gaps: This applies to an ideal additive two-quote abstraction. It does not rule out multi-quote codebooks, nonsymmetric designs, or constructions whose realized market reward curves do not decompose into independent signed block advantages.
   690	Source: `iterations/iter_013/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_013/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
