   703	## Lemma: Solvency invariant for delayed advantage fast safety
   704	Status: proved
   705	Assumptions: Delayed full-information two-arm advantage abstraction; \(\Delta_t=s_t-f_t\in[-1,1]\); slow-play probabilities \(q_t\in[0,1]\) are predictable from information available before round \(t\); \(\Delta_u\) is observed by time \(u+D\) regardless of whether slow was played. At the beginning of round \(t\),
   706	\[
   707	O_t=\sum_{u\le t-D}q_u\Delta_u,\qquad
   708	P_t=\sum_{t-D<u<t}q_u,\qquad C_t=B+O_t-P_t .
   709	\]
   710	Statement: If the learner always chooses \(q_t\le [C_t]_+\), then for every realized \(\Delta_{1:T}\),
   711	\[
   712	A_T=\sum_{t=1}^Tq_t\Delta_t\ge -B.
   713	\]
   714	Equivalently, expected regret to the fast arm satisfies \(R_F=-A_T\le B\).
   715	Proof sketch: Maintain \(C_t\ge0\). When pending exposure \(q_u\) is revealed, \(O\) increases by \(q_u\Delta_u\) and \(P\) decreases by \(q_u\), so \(C\) changes by \(q_u(\Delta_u+1)\ge0\). Adding new exposure \(q_t\le C_t\) decreases \(C\) by at most \(q_t\) and leaves it nonnegative. At the end, unrevealed pending terms can hurt by at most their total exposure:
   716	\[
   717	A_T=O_{\rm end}+\sum_{\rm pending}q_u\Delta_u
   718	\ge O_{\rm end}-P_{\rm end}\ge -B .
   719	\]
   720	Known gaps: This is only the fast-safety half of the two-arm target. It says nothing by itself about \(S_T-A_T\), and it is formulated for action probabilities/expected rewards rather than realized Bernoulli plays.
   721	Source: `iterations/iter_015/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_015/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   722	
   723	## Lemma: D-scale slow-regret term is unavoidable under delayed advantage indistinguishability
   724	Status: proved for the repaired common-baseline test
   725	Assumptions: Two-arm delayed advantage model with no information distinguishing two environments for the first \(D\) rounds. One convenient realization is \(f_t\equiv1/2\) and \(\Delta_t=\pm1/2\) for \(t\le D\), so fast rewards are identical and slow advantages are delayed.
   726	Statement: If an algorithm has fast regret at most \(B\) on the all-negative environment over the first \(D\) rounds, then on the all-positive environment its slow regret over the same prefix is at least \(D/2-B\).
   727	Proof sketch: On \(\Delta_t=-1/2\), fast regret over the first \(D\) rounds is \(\frac12\sum_{t\le D}q_t\), so the fast-regret bound implies \(\sum_{t\le D}q_t\le2B\). The all-positive environment is indistinguishable for the first \(D\) decisions under the common baseline, so the same \(q_t\)'s are used. Slow regret there is
   728	\[
   729	\frac12\sum_{t\le D}(1-q_t)\ge D/2-B.
   730	\]
   731	Known gaps: This is only a lower-bound stress test on the necessary scale of the slow term. The explorer's original all-\(\pm1\) version is invalid in the original fast/slow reward model if immediate fast rewards differ and reveal the environment.
   732	Source: `iterations/iter_015/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_015/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   733	
   734	## Lemma: Periodic finite-grid market refutes the simultaneous expected nested oracle
   735	Status: proved for the stated finite-grid oracle
   736	Assumptions: Deterministic public-exogenous path; finite grid \(G=\{\ell,h\}\) with \(h=\ell+d<1\); choose \(m\in(\ell,h)\); windows \(H_1=1,H_2=D\); horizon \(T=ND+1\); high spikes above \(h\) occur at times \(jD+1\), \(j=0,\ldots,N\), and all other prices equal \(m\). Assume \(\ell D\ge h\). Consider the simultaneous expected oracle
   737	\[
   738	V_m^G-\mathbb E L^m(A)\le B_m+c(V_2^G-V_m^G),\qquad m=1,2.
   739	\]
   740	Statement: For fixed \(c\) and standard budgets \(B_1=O(\sqrt{ND})\), \(B_2=O(D\sqrt N)\), the oracle fails for suitable \(d,D,N\).
   741	Proof sketch: On active rounds \(1,\ldots,ND\), low earns short and long reward \(\ell\) every round. High earns long reward \(h\) every round, but short reward only on the \(N\) rounds immediately preceding high spikes. Therefore
   742	\[
   743	V_1^G=\ell ND,\qquad V_2^G=hND,\qquad V_2^G-V_1^G=dND.
   744	\]
   745	Let \(M\) be the expected number of active high quotes. Long regret is \(d(ND-M)\), so the \(m=2\) oracle forces \(M\ge ND-B_2/d\). Since at most \(N\) high quotes earn short reward,
   746	\[
   747	V_1^G-\mathbb E L^1
   748	\ge \ell M-hN
   749	\ge \ell ND-\ell B_2/d-hN.
   750	\]
   751	Choose \(d\ll \ell/c\), \(D\gg h/\ell\), and \(N\gg d^{-2}\) (with constants absorbing the budget bounds). Then this lower bound is larger than \(B_1+c\,dND\), contradicting the \(m=1\) oracle.
   752	Known gaps: This targets a strong simultaneous expected oracle, not every possible adaptive-truncation algorithm. The statement is finite-grid; dense-grid/continuum variants need separate analysis because intermediate quotes may change constants.
   753	Source: `iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_015/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   754	
   755	## Lemma: Horizon-censored future maximum convention
   756	Status: proved / notation correction
   757	Assumptions: Finite horizon \(T\); reward \(r_t(a)=a{\bf 1}\{\exists s\in\{t+1,\ldots,T\}:P_s>a\}\).
   758	Statement: Every finite-horizon reward-curve, Bayes-envelope, and payoff-measure formula using a future maximum must use
   759	\[
   760	M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,
   761	\]
   762	with the empty maximum at \(t=T\) interpreted as producing zero reward. Equivalently, \(a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\}\) denotes \(0\) when there is no future time \(s\le T\).
   763	Proof sketch: By definition, a quote posted at time \(t\) is rewarded over horizon \(T\) only if it trades at some future time \(s\le T\). Prices after \(T\) do not enter \(r_t\), truncation, comparator values, Bayes envelopes, or payoff-weighted measures. Using \(\max_{s>t}P_s\) for an infinite continuation can count post-horizon hits and changes the game.
   764	Known gaps: Purely notational, but important for theorem statements. Infinite-process tail examples may still define post-horizon continuations for \(\beta\) calculations; finite-horizon regret formulas must remain censored at \(T\).
   765	Source: `iterations/iter_016/solution_critic_02.md`; `iterations/iter_016/solution_gate.md`; `iterations/iter_016/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
