   111	## Tiny-price long-delay blocks break raw beta without meaningful regret
   112	Construction: Infinite deterministic sequence. For \(m\ge1\), let \(L_m=2^m\), \(p_m=2^{-2m}\), and \(S_m=\sum_{j=1}^m(L_j+1)=2^{m+1}-2+m\). Block \(m\) has zeros at times \(S_{m-1}+1,\ldots,S_m-1\), then spike \(P_{S_m}=p_m\).
   113	Facts: For every finite \(h\), choose \(m\) with \(L_m>h\), start at the first zero of block \(m\), and quote \(a=p_m/2\). The first future price above \(a\) is the spike after \(L_m\) steps, so the infinite-process raw \(\beta(h)=1\). But for any horizon \(T\) and quote \(a\), let \(M=\max\{m:S_m\le T,\ p_m>a\}\), if nonempty. Since \(p_m\) decreases strictly, only quote times \(t<S_M\) can trade by horizon \(T\), hence
   114	\[
   115	\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)=a(S_M-1)<p_M(S_M-1)
   116	=2^{-2M}(2^{M+1}+M-3)\le 1/2.
   117	\]
   118	If no such \(M\) exists, the reward is zero. Thus \(\sup_T\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)\le1/2\), and the zero-quote learner has regret at most \(1/2\) for every horizon.
   119	Iteration 7 strengthening: for integer \(H\),
   120	\[
   121	\sup_T\Delta_T(H)\le C/H.
   122	\]
   123	For \(a=0\) the loss is zero. For a fixed quote \(a>0\), let \(M=\max\{m:p_m>a\}\). Lost \(H\)-truncated reward is at most
   124	\[
   125	a\sum_{j\le M}(L_j+1-H)_+
   126	\le
   127	p_M\sum_{j\le M:L_j+1>H}(L_j+1)
   128	\le C\,4^{-M}2^M\le C/H,
   129	\]
   130	with zero loss if \(L_M+1\le H\).
   131	Why it matters: Raw hitting-delay probability ignores reward magnitude. Long delays can be certain only at quotes whose payoff scale is too small to create meaningful regret, and in fact value-weighted truncation is small.
   132	Status: Valid infinite-process/raw-\(\beta\) counterexample. For finite-horizon \(\beta_T\), state the result as failure of any uniform raw-tail necessity rather than \(\beta_T(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
   133	Source: `iterations/iter_006/solver_attempt.md`; `iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/solver_attempt.md`; synthesis.
   134	
   135	## Infinite-class compactness/measurability gap
   136	Construction: Abstractly, take \(\Theta=\mathbb N\), \(V_\theta=1\), and attainable reward vectors
   137	\[
   138	C=\{c\in[0,1]^\mathbb N:c\text{ has finite support}\}.
   139	\]
   140	Every finite subclass can be controlled with value \(0\), but the full value is \(1\).
   141	Market-flavored stress test: Let \(A\subset[0,1]\) be non-Borel. With \(T=2\), \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\), world \(x\) has \(P_1=x\) and \(P_2=1\) if \(x\in A\), \(P_2=1/2\) otherwise. Finite subclasses can be memorized by Borel policies, but a full-class Borel policy with regret \(<1/6\) would need to separate \(A\) by a Borel set.
   142	Iteration 9 linear amplification: For \(T=D+1\), set \(P_1=x\), \(P_2=\cdots=P_D=0\), and \(P_{D+1}=1\) on \(A\), \(P_{D+1}=1/2\) off \(A\). On \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\), every finite subclass still has value \(0\), but the full Borel-policy value is \(D/6=(T-1)/6\). If \(q_t(x)\) is the Borel probability of quoting \(2/3\) on early round \(t\), then
   143	\[
   144	R_A(x)=\frac{D-\sum_t q_t(x)}3,\qquad
   145	R_{A^c}(x)=\frac{\sum_t q_t(x)}3.
   146	\]
   147	Regret \(<D/6\) everywhere would make \(A=\{x:D^{-1}\sum_tq_t(x)>1/2\}\) Borel, while \(q_t\equiv1/2\) attains \(D/6\).
   148	Why it matters: The finite-subclass Bayes modulus is not automatically a full infinite-class characterization. It characterizes the product-closure relaxation unless the attainable reward set is product closed.
   149	Status: Valid as a compactness/measurability obstruction. The market-flavored example is pathological and depends on Borel-policy restrictions; it disappears if arbitrary nonmeasurable policies are allowed or if \(A\) is Borel.
   150	Iteration 8 refinement: payoff-weighted reward-measure domination fails in the non-Borel label example because the relevant class contains uncountably many singular Dirac-type prefix/reward measures. Thus it remains a stress test for any unrestricted infinite-class theorem. Iteration 9 found no natural Borel-label version; identifiable Borel and countable-label variants are easy by direct decoding.
   151	Source: `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_008/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_009/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_009/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   152	
   153	## Weak/Feller continuity is not a no-gap condition by itself
   154	Construction/stress test: Let \(\Theta=[0,1]\) and take deterministic prefixes \(P_1=\theta\). Such classes can be weakly compact and Feller-continuous while the prefix laws \(\{\delta_\theta\}\) are mutually singular and not total-variation separable.
   155	Facts: In the simple identifiable example \(T=2\), \(P_1=\theta\), \(P_2=1\), the class is easy for a Borel policy because the parameter is publicly revealed. However, finite interpolation can product-converge to arbitrary functions of \(\theta\), including non-Borel ones, so product-closedness of the Borel attainable reward set is not implied by weak/Feller continuity alone.
   156	Why it matters: Failure of product closedness and high regret are distinct. Identifiability may solve some nondominated classes, but it is a different mechanism from compactness and does not justify a general no-gap theorem.
   157	Status: Valid stress test against weak-continuity-only no-gap claims; not a lower-bound example by itself.
   158	Source: `iterations/iter_008/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_008/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   159	
   160	## Action-dependent prices obstruct full-information reduction
   161	Construction idea: Let future prices depend on the learner's current or future quotes.
   162	Facts: Then counterfactual rewards \(r_t^H(b)\) for unplayed grid quotes may not be determined by the realized future price path, or may correspond to a different path under a comparator policy.
   163	Why it matters: The upper-bound theorem is not valid in this model without new causal definitions.
   164	Status: Model obstruction, not a numeric counterexample yet.
   165	Source: idea_01 critique.
   166	
   167	## Candidate anytime obstruction via two deadlines
   168	Construction idea: Deterministic known infinite sequence with super-growing blocks: \(N_m\) zeros, then price \(1/2\), then \(N_m\) zeros, then price \(1\), with \(N_m\) dominating all previous lengths.
   169	Facts sketched by critic: For the first \(N_m\) quotes of such a pair, the benchmark by the early deadline is about \(N_m/2\), while the benchmark by the later deadline is about \(N_m\). For any horizon-free quotes \(a_i\),
   170	\[
   171	A=N_m/2-\sum_i a_i{\bf 1}\{a_i<1/2\},\qquad
   172	B=N_m-\sum_i a_i
   173	\]
   174	satisfy \(A+B\ge N_m/2\), so one of the two deadlines has regret at least \(N_m/4\) from that block.
   175	Why it matters: The known-law oracle lemma depends on knowing the evaluation horizon. A single anytime policy may not inherit the \(O(1)\) guarantee.
   176	Status: Plausible stress test; needs a fully formal infinite-process and horizon-subsequence proof.
   177	Source: `iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   178	
   179	## Completed-kernel null-set stress test
   180	Construction idea: Let a completed-measurable finite-grid kernel differ from all Borel versions on a \(\lambda_t\)-null non-Borel set that some prefix law \(P^\theta_{1:t}\) may charge. If only payoff-weighted measures \(\mu^\theta_{t,a}\) are dominated by \(\lambda_t\), then the prefix law itself need not ignore that set.
   181	Facts: Borelizing the kernel can change the policy's behavior, future internal state, or pathwise feedback on such prefix histories. Under public exogenous prices and additive expected rewards, this is harmless for the no-gap theorem only because the reward coordinates use
   182	\[
   183	\int q_{t,a}\,d\mu^\theta_{t,a},
   184	\]
   185	and \(\mu^\theta_{t,a}\ll\lambda_t\) makes all changes on \(\lambda_t\)-null sets invisible to expected payoff.
   186	Why it matters: The iteration 10 Borel implementation lemma should be stated as reward-vector preservation, not behavioral equivalence. It also explains why exogeneity and additive rewards are essential: if actions affected future prices or private feedback carried extra information, changing behavior on a prefix-law-positive null set could matter.
   187	Status: Valid stress test for theorem wording; not a counterexample to payoff-weighted domination because the theorem only needs payoff-coordinate preservation.
   188	Iteration 16 status: This remains the key stress test for upgrading from completed \(L^\infty\) prefix kernels to original Borel policies. The upgrade can only claim reward-coordinate equivalence after proving both inclusions \(C_{\rm Borel}=C_{L^\infty}\); it must not claim behavioral or pathwise equivalence.
   189	Source: `iterations/iter_010/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_010/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_016/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_016/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
