   191	## Policy-alignment assumption stress tests
   192	Construction ideas:
   193	- Law-by-law policy versions: each singleton law may choose a convenient a.s. representative, but the representatives need not assemble into one common Borel policy on the full public history space.
   194	- Private side signal: two worlds have identical public price laws, but a private learner signal reveals \(\theta\) before decisions.
   195	- Law-correlated randomization: the learner's private seed is correlated with the active law.
   196	- Nonadditive/action-dependent market: current quotes affect future prices or rewards include inventory, budget, queue priority, or other cross-time state.
   197	
   198	Facts: In these variants, the one-time law-independent public-prefix marginal \(q_t(P_{1:t})\) may not exist or may not preserve expected reward coordinates. With a private side signal or law-correlated seed, action marginals conditional on \(P_{1:t}\) can depend on \(\theta\). With action-dependent prices or nonadditive rewards, a marginal at time \(t\) is insufficient because actions alter future paths or rewards.
   199	
   200	Why it matters: The iteration 12 forward alignment lemma is valid only for a common Borel finite-grid public-exogenous seed/state policy model with independent private randomness, full public observation, reconstructible feedback, and additive quote rewards.
   201	
   202	Status: Model-boundary obstructions, not counterexamples to the stated finite-grid public-exogenous alignment lemma.
   203	Source: `iterations/iter_012/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_012/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   204	
   205	## Hidden/labeled tail-profile no-go pair
   206	Construction: Fix \(T=D+1\), \(q\in(0,1]\), and two terminal laws
   207	\[
   208	Y_L\sim(1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[1/2,5/8],\qquad
   209	Y_H\sim(1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[3/4,7/8].
   210	\]
   211	Hidden class:
   212	\[
   213	P_1=c,\quad P_2=\cdots=P_D=0,\quad P_{D+1}=Y_\theta.
   214	\]
   215	Labeled class:
   216	\[
   217	P_1=\ell_\theta,\quad P_2=\cdots=P_D=0,\quad P_{D+1}=Y_\theta,\qquad \ell_L\ne\ell_H.
   218	\]
   219	Facts: Under the stated protocol there is no quote before \(P_1\), and a quote at time \(t\) trades only against future prices \(P_s\), \(s>t\). Thus changing \(P_1\) to a label does not affect any legal quote reward or delay in a fixed world. The hidden and labeled versions have identical per-world reward curves, comparator values, \(\beta\), \(\Delta\), and \(\delta\). With absorbing prices after \(T\), \(\beta_\theta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) and \(0\) for \(h\ge D\); for integer \(0\le H<D\),
   220	\[
   221	\Delta_L(H)=\delta_L(H)=q(D-H)/2,\qquad
   222	\Delta_H(H)=\delta_H(H)=3q(D-H)/4.
   223	\]
   224	However, the hidden class inherits the q-scaled hidden-terminal lower bound \(qD/8\), while in the labeled class the learner observes \(\theta\) before \(a_1\) and can play the active world's optimum. Continuum regret is zero; grid regret is zero if the grid contains \(1/2\) and \(3/4\), and otherwise only has the usual discretization loss.
   225	Why it matters: This refutes any unknown-class learnability criterion based only on individual laws' scalar delay/truncation profiles or their envelopes. The missing ingredient is public observability/predictability of the active reward curve.
   226	Status: Valid under public exogenous prices, no pre-\(P_1\) quote, and no same-period trade. It does not refute criteria involving posterior information, Bayes gaps, public-prefix laws, or the full statistical experiment.
   227	Source: `iterations/iter_011/solver_attempt.md`; `iterations/iter_011/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_011/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   228	
   229	## Same-level repeated atoms break the intended tail envelope
   230	Construction idea: Repeat \(N\) length-\(D\) q-scaled hidden-terminal blocks at the same quote levels. Each block has a terminal spike event with probability \(q\), and old quotes remain live after their own block.
   231	Facts: A quote posted in the first block at a threshold below the spike level, conditional on not being hit in its own terminal event, can still be hit by any later block's spike. Thus
   232	\[
   233	\Pr(D<\tau<\infty)
   234	\approx
   235	(1-q)(1-(1-q)^{N-1}),
   236	\]
   237	which is near one when \(Nq\gg1\), not order \(q\).
   238	Why it matters: A lower-bound construction meant to obey a power envelope \(B(D)\approx q\) cannot simply paste q-scaled atoms at the same quote levels. Persistent quotes are a core feature of the protocol and must be accounted for before claiming any tail profile.
   239	Status: Valid stress test against naive repeated-block lower bounds; not a complete lower-bound counterexample.
   240	Source: `iterations/iter_013/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   241	
   242	## Independent hidden bits collapse against a single fixed comparator
   243	Construction idea: In an ideal additive two-quote block model, each block \(j\) has independent sign \(\sigma_j\in\{\pm1\}\) and contributes relative advantage \(w_j\sigma_j\) to one of the two quotes.
   244	Facts: The best fixed quote's expected advantage is
   245	\[
   246	\mathbb E\left|\sum_j w_j\sigma_j\right|
   247	\le
   248	\left(\sum_jw_j^2\right)^{1/2}.
   249	\]
   250	Equal block weights therefore give \(\Theta(w\sqrt N)\), not \(\Theta(Nw)\).
   251	Why it matters: Independent hidden block types avoid one-reveal learning, but they also destroy linear accumulation because the benchmark is one fixed quote, not the per-block best quote.
   252	Status: Valid obstruction in the additive abstraction. Needs separate work to rule out multi-quote/codebook market constructions.
   253	Source: `iterations/iter_013/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_013/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   254	
   255	## Max-delay contamination for adaptive truncation
   256	Construction idea: Include dyadic truncation-window experts up to \(H=T\) in a single delayed-Hedge master.
   257	Facts: Standard delayed-feedback analyses with a common maximum delay pay overhead roughly \(\sqrt{T H_{\max}\log N}\). With \(H_{\max}=T\), the overhead is essentially linear even if the best analysis window is small.
   258	Why it matters: A tail-adaptive truncation theorem needs comparator-specific delay dependence or extra structure; the accepted fixed-\(H\) residue-Hedge lemma cannot be used as-is.
   259	Status: Algorithmic obstruction/stress test, not an impossibility theorem. A heterogeneous-delay experts lemma may still be true.
   260	Source: `iterations/iter_013/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   261	
   262	## Delayed advantage safety/capture stress tests
   263	Construction idea: In the two-arm fast/slow delayed experts abstraction, write \(\Delta_t=s_t-f_t\), \(q_t=\Pr(\text{slow})\), and \(A_T=\sum_tq_t\Delta_t\). Fast regret is \(-A_T\), slow regret is \(S_T-A_T\), where \(S_T=\sum_t\Delta_t\).
   264	Facts:
   265	- If \(\Delta_t=-1\) for all \(t\), fast-regret \(O(\sqrt T)\) forces \(\sum_tq_t=O(\sqrt T)\).
   266	- If \(\Delta_t=1\) for all \(t\), slow-regret \(O(\sqrt{TD})\) forces \(\sum_t(1-q_t)=O(\sqrt{TD})\).
   267	- A bait-and-switch sequence with a long positive observed prefix followed by \(D\) hidden negative advantages attacks policies that raise slow exposure based only on observed counterfactual slow rewards rather than banked advantage.
   268	Why it matters: These tests isolate the unresolved safety/capture tradeoff in comparator-specific delayed experts. They do not refute the desired theorem, but any candidate algorithm must pass them.
   269	Status: Valid stress tests; no impossibility theorem yet.
   270	Source: `iterations/iter_014/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_014/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   271	
   272	## Pathwise nested-window charge can exceed expected truncation gap
   273	Construction idea: Compare a pathwise full-window best quote to an expected fixed comparator. In a one-terminal-price example with \(H=0\), the pathwise full-window best quote can adapt to the realized terminal price, while \(\delta_T^P(H)\) uses \(\sup_b b\Pr(Y>b)\) in expectation.
   274	Facts: For equal-revenue-type terminal distributions, \(\mathbb E[\max_b b{\bf 1}\{Y>b\}]\) can exceed \(\sup_b b\Pr(Y>b)\) by logarithmic factors. Thus a pathwise charge \(\mathbb E[V_M^G(\omega)-V_m^G(\omega)]\) is not controlled by the expected comparator-gap truncation loss \(\delta_T^P(H)\).
   275	Why it matters: Nested-window adaptive truncation proofs must use one value convention consistently. A pathwise oracle may be true but would prove a bound with a stronger, different charge than the accepted \(\delta_T^P(H)\).
   276	Status: Valid stress test against pathwise/expected mixing.
   277	Source: `iterations/iter_014/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   278	
   279	## Horizon cutoff for nested-window increments
   280	Construction idea: Take \(t\) near \(T\), no price above \(b\) before \(T\), but a post-horizon price above \(b\).
   281	Facts: The formula \(z_t^m(b)=b{\bf 1}\{H_{m-1}<\tau_t(b)\le H_m\}\) would count this as a late increment if the post-horizon hit lies within \(H_m\). The finite-horizon reward should be zero because rewards only count hits with \(\tau_t(b)\le T-t\).
   282	Why it matters: Any nested-window decomposition must use horizon-censored increments
   283	\[
   284	z_t^m(b)=b{\bf 1}\{H_{m-1}<\tau_t(b)\le H_m,\ \tau_t(b)\le T-t\}.
   285	\]
   286	Status: Valid correction to the iteration 14 nested-window formula.
   287	Source: `iterations/iter_014/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   288	
   289	## Budget-depletion stress test for safe-clipped delayed Hedge
   290	Construction idea: In the delayed full-information advantage abstraction, let a proposal \(p_t=1/2\) before any feedback. Choose \(D>2B\) and set
   291	\[
   292	\Delta_1=\cdots=\Delta_{2B}=-1,\qquad
   293	\Delta_{2B+1}=\cdots=\Delta_T=+1.
   294	\]
   295	Use the clipped solvency rule \(q_t=\min\{p_t,C_t,1\}\), where
   296	\[
   297	C_t=B+\sum_{u\le t-D}q_u\Delta_u-\sum_{t-D<u<t}q_u.
   298	\]
   299	Facts: Before feedback arrives, the rule plays \(q_t=1/2\) for \(2B\) rounds and spends all capacity. The subsequent negative reveals do not restore capacity because each reveal changes capacity by \(q_t(\Delta_t+1)=0\). Since unplayed positive advantages do not enter the banked payoff, the rule can stay at \(q_t=0\) even after the positive regime is observed. Thus \(A_T=-B\) but \(S_T-A_T\) is linear.
   300	Why it matters: The solvency invariant is useful, but naive clipping can destroy slow capture. Any delayed wealth or reserve algorithm must avoid permanent zero-capacity traps.
   301	Status: Valid refutation of the exact safe-clipped delayed-Hedge sketch, not of all two-arm delayed-advantage algorithms.
   302	Source: `iterations/iter_015/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_015/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   303	
   304	## Periodic finite-grid market obstruction to simultaneous nested oracle
   305	Construction: Fix \(G=\{\ell,h\}\), \(h=\ell+d<1\), choose \(m\in(\ell,h)\), and windows \(H_1=1,H_2=D\). Let \(T=ND+1\). Put high spikes above \(h\) at times \(jD+1\), \(j=0,\ldots,N\), and set every other price to \(m\). Assume \(\ell D\ge h\).
   306	Facts: On active rounds \(1,\ldots,ND\), low earns short and long reward \(\ell\) every round; high earns long reward \(h\) every round; high earns short reward only on the \(N\) rounds immediately before high spikes. Hence
   307	\[
   308	V_1^G=\ell ND,\qquad V_2^G=hND,\qquad V_2^G-V_1^G=dND.
   309	\]
   310	If \(M\) is the expected number of high quotes and long-window regret is at most \(B_2\), then \(M\ge ND-B_2/d\). Short-window regret is at least
   311	\[
   312	\ell M-hN\ge \ell ND-\ell B_2/d-hN.
   313	\]
   314	For any fixed oracle constant \(c\), taking \(d\ll\ell/c\), then \(D\gg h/\ell\), then \(N\gg d^{-2}\) makes this exceed \(B_1+c\,dND\) for standard \(B_1=O(\sqrt{ND})\), \(B_2=O(D\sqrt N)\).
   315	Why it matters: This refutes a strong simultaneous expected nested-window oracle that tries to control every window by \(B_m+c(V_2^G-V_m^G)\). The obstruction is deterministic, so there is no pathwise/expected ambiguity in this calculation.
   316	Status: Valid finite-grid stress test. It does not rule out weaker min-over-window adaptive truncation bounds, heterogeneous-delay routes, or continuum variants after adding intermediate quotes.
   317	Source: `iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_015/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   318	
   319	## Finite-support reward vectors separate ordinary priors from `ba` priors
   320	Construction: Abstract fixed-grid reward-vector example. Let \(\Theta=\mathbb N\), \(V_\theta\equiv1\), and
   321	\[
   322	C=\{c\in[0,1]^\mathbb N:c\text{ has finite support}\}.
   323	\]
   324	Facts: The true full-class value is \(1\), because every finite-support \(c\) misses some coordinate \(\theta\) and hence \(\sup_\theta(1-c_\theta)=1\). Every finite subclass has value \(0\), because one can choose \(c=1\) on that finite set. More strongly, every countably additive probability \(\pi\) on \(\mathbb N\) gives ordinary-prior dual value \(0\), since finite supports can capture arbitrarily large \(\pi\)-mass:
   325	\[
   326	\sup_{c\in C}\langle c,\pi\rangle=1.
   327	\]
   328	A free-ultrafilter finitely additive probability \(\nu\) with \(\nu(F)=0\) for every finite \(F\) gives
   329	\[
   330	\langle V,\nu\rangle=1,\qquad \sup_{c\in C}\langle c,\nu\rangle=0,
   331	\]
   332	and witnesses the full value in the `ba` dual.
   333	Why it matters: Finite/countably additive priors recover only the finite-subclass/product-closure relaxation, while finitely additive `ba` priors can detect true infinite-class gaps. This explains why the iteration 19 dual is exact but non-statistical.
   334	Status: Valid abstract reward-set obstruction. It is not by itself a natural market class; the non-Borel label market construction is the market-flavored measurability analogue already recorded above.
   335	Source: `iterations/iter_019/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_019/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_019/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_019/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   336	
   337	## Nonconvex deterministic-policy stress test for the `ba` dual
   338	Construction: Abstract reward-vector game with \(\Theta=\{1,2\}\), comparator vector \(V=(1,1)\), and deterministic-policy reward set
   339	\[
   340	C=\{(1,0),(0,1)\}.
   341	\]
   342	Facts: The deterministic primal value is
   343	\[
   344	\inf_{c\in C}\max_\theta(1-c_\theta)=1.
   345	\]
   346	If ex-ante randomization is allowed, the convexified point \((1/2,1/2)\) gives value \(1/2\). The support-function dual over a convex reward set therefore characterizes the randomized/convexified value, not the deterministic-policy value.
   347	Why it matters: The arbitrary-class `ba` dual requires a nonempty convex \(C\), supplied in the market model by ex-ante private randomization between common policies. Without this assumption, the claimed minimax equality can be false for the intended deterministic policy class.
   348	Status: Valid abstract stress test for theorem assumptions.
   349	Source: `iterations/iter_020/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.

